-- title:
--   Pull-back of 𝒪(mp Z) is trivial off the support
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ and $Y$ be schemes, let $I$ be an ideal sheaf datum on $Y$, and assume `I.IsInvertible`, i.e. every point of $Y$ lies in a basic open $Y_g$ of an affine open $U \subseteq Y$ such that the ideal attached by $I$ to the affine open $Y_g$ is generated by a single non-zero-divisor of $\Gamma(Y, Y_g)$. Let $f \colon X \to Y$ be a morphism of schemes whose image on points is disjoint from the support of $I$: the hypothesis is that $\mathrm{range}(f.\mathrm{base})$ and the support of $I$ are disjoint as subsets of the underlying space of $Y$. Let $B$ be a sheaf of modules on $Y$ (an object of `Y.Modules`) which is assumed to be either `I.module` — the kernel of the canonical map from the unit module $\mathcal O_Y$ to the pushforward along the closed immersion `I.subschemeι` of the unit module of the closed subscheme cut out by $I$ — or `I.invModule`, the internal-hom dual $(\mathrm{ihom}\,I.\mathrm{module})(\mathbf 1)$ of that module. The conclusion is that the type of isomorphisms $(\text{pullback } f)(B) \cong \mathbf 1_{X.\mathrm{Modules}}$ in $X.\mathrm{Modules}$ is non-empty, where $\mathbf 1_{X.\mathrm{Modules}}$ is the unit module, i.e. the structure sheaf $\mathcal O_X$ viewed as a sheaf of modules over itself. Thus the assertion is the existence of an isomorphism, with no canonical choice singled out, and it is stated uniformly for a module $B$ known only to be one of the two.
--
--   In classical language: if $Z \subseteq Y$ is the closed subscheme cut out by an invertible ideal sheaf $I$, and $f \colon X \to Y$ has image disjoint from the support of $I$, then both $f^{*}\mathcal O_Y(-Z)$ and $f^{*}\mathcal O_Y(Z)$ are isomorphic to $\mathcal O_X$. The formal version differs from the textbook statement in two ways: invertibility of $I$ is the local-generation-by-a-non-zero-divisor condition recorded by `IsInvertible` rather than invertibility of an associated line bundle, and the two cases $\mathcal O(-Z)$ and $\mathcal O(Z)$ are merged into a single hypothesis that $B$ equals `I.module` or `I.invModule`. It is used in the construction of rigidified line bundles on the generic fibre of an arithmetic surface attached to a modular curve, where ideal sheaves of components of a closed fibre have support disjoint from the image of the morphism in question, so their associated twists become trivial after pull-back.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_Scheme_IdealSheafData_nonempty_pullback_obj_iso_unit_of_disjoint_range_support_of_eq_module_or_eq_invModule.lean

